From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Faust <willtechnoduck@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 18:28:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] iOS: fix off-by-8 in ret_sp_misaligned for variadic ARM64EC
 entry thunks

An ARM64EC variadic entry thunk reads x4 as its input -- the x64 caller's
stack pointer -- and advances it past the 32-byte home space:

  $ientry_thunk$cdecl$i8$varargs:  add x4, x4, #0x20 ; mov x5, xzr ; blr x9

The normal path pops the return address first (`ldr lr, [x4], #0x8`),
leaving x4 = RSP+8, so the thunk lands on the first stack argument.
ret_sp_misaligned deliberately LEAVES the return address on the stack to
preserve 16-byte alignment, but still passed x4 = RSP -- one slot short,
shifting every stack argument by 8 bytes.

Only variadic callees are affected, since non-variadic entry thunks never
read x4, which is why this stayed hidden outside RPC.

Not iOS-specific: any variadic ARM64EC function called from x64 with a
16-byte-aligned RSP would hit it.

Also adds a probe naming why an unaligned 128-bit CAS is fatal. RunCASPAL
implements ONLY Size==0 (32-bit pairs); Size==1 (a 128-bit CAS from x86
LOCK CMPXCHG16B) falls through to `return false`, which callers report as
"Unhandled JIT SIGBUS CASPAL" and escalate to STATUS_DATATYPE_MISALIGNMENT.
Steam/CEF now reaches that path. Before implementing a 128-bit unaligned
CAS the probe establishes whether the address is genuinely misaligned --
a correct x86 program cannot issue an unaligned LOCK CMPXCHG16B, so a
computed-wrong address is the competing explanation and needs a different
fix entirely.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Madeira-commit: fdf361f0ec3e0f86b8313a8c3fad360146a1bd90
Rebased: clean
Class: madeira-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-09-24-fex-latest-rebase.md
Offered-upstream: no
---
 FEXCore/Source/Utils/ArchHelpers/Arm64.cpp | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.S            | 13 +++++++++++
 2 files changed, 39 insertions(+)

diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Utils/ArchHelpers/Arm64.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Utils/ArchHelpers/Arm64.cpp
index 01e5bb2..4c8ae32 100644
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Utils/ArchHelpers/Arm64.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Utils/ArchHelpers/Arm64.cpp
@@ -521,6 +521,31 @@ static bool RunCASPAL(uint64_t* GPRs, uint32_t Size, uint32_t DesiredReg1, uint3
   return false;
 }
 
+/* iOS-Mythic ml220 PROBE. RunCASPAL implements ONLY Size==0 (32-bit pairs); Size==1
+ * (64-bit pairs, i.e. a 128-bit CAS from x86 LOCK CMPXCHG16B) falls straight through to
+ * `return false`, which callers report as "Unhandled JIT SIGBUS CASPAL" and escalate to a
+ * fatal STATUS_DATATYPE_MISALIGNMENT. Steam/CEF hits this
+ *   Unhandled JIT SIGBUS CASPAL: PC: 0x120e53c00 Instruction: 0x4866fd64
+ *     = CASPAL x6,x7, x4,x5, [x11]   (sz=1)
+ *
+ * Implementing a 128-bit unaligned CAS is real work, so establish WHY the address is
+ * unaligned first: a correct x86 program cannot issue an unaligned LOCK CMPXCHG16B (it
+ * #GPs on hardware), so either the guest really does it -- and we must emulate -- or the
+ * address we computed is wrong, which is a different bug entirely and would make the
+ * emulation pointless. Print the address and its misalignment so the next run decides. */
+static void IosLogUnimplementedCASPAL(uint32_t Size, uint64_t* GPRs, uint32_t AddressReg) {
+  static int reports;
+
+  if (Size == 0 || reports >= 8) {
+    return;
+  }
+  reports++;
+  LogMan::Msg::EFmt("[caspal128] UNIMPLEMENTED Size={} addrReg=x{} addr={:#x} misalign={} "
+                    "crosses16B={}",
+                    Size, AddressReg, GPRs[AddressReg], GPRs[AddressReg] & 15,
+                    (GPRs[AddressReg] & 15) ? "yes" : "no");
+}
+
 static bool HandleCASPAL(uint32_t Instr, uint64_t* GPRs, uint32_t* StrictSplitLockMutex) {
   uint32_t Size = (Instr >> 30) & 1;
 
@@ -530,6 +555,7 @@ static bool HandleCASPAL(uint32_t Instr, uint64_t* GPRs, uint32_t* StrictSplitLo
   uint32_t ExpectedReg2 = ExpectedReg1 + 1;
   uint32_t AddressReg = (Instr >> 5) & 0b11111;
 
+  IosLogUnimplementedCASPAL(Size, GPRs, AddressReg);
   return RunCASPAL(GPRs, Size, DesiredReg1, DesiredReg2, ExpectedReg1, ExpectedReg2, AddressReg, StrictSplitLockMutex);
 }
 
diff --git a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.S b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.S
index acaf029..0076cac 100644
--- a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.S
+++ b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.S
@@ -165,6 +165,19 @@ ret_sp_misaligned:
   // In the case of the x64 caller leaving sp only 8-byte aligned, leave the return address on the stack to keep 16-byte
   // alignment and have the callee return to an x86 ret instruction. FEX can then return to the actual caller keeping
   // the misaligned RSP.
+  //
+  // ml218: x4 must still point PAST the return address. On the path above the address is
+  // popped (`ldr lr, [x4], #0x8`), leaving x4 = RSP+8 -- the base of the x64 home space,
+  // which a variadic entry thunk then advances by 0x20 to reach the first stack argument:
+  //   $ientry_thunk$cdecl$i8$varargs:  add x4, x4, #0x20
+  // Here the address is deliberately LEFT on the stack, so x4 = RSP is one slot short and
+  // every stack argument is read 8 bytes early. Measured directly in a Steam RPC call:
+  //   [ndr-shift] param3 off=0x18 StackTop=0x703CD5EBF0 HERE=0x10 next=0x703CD5EC88
+  //               => SHIFT: real pointer is ONE SLOT LATER (+8)
+  // which is how ccontext arrived as 0x10 and faulted in #NdrContextHandleUnmarshall+0xcc.
+  // Only variadic callees are affected -- non-variadic entry thunks never read x4 -- which
+  // is why this stayed hidden outside RPC. sp is intentionally left untouched.
+  add x4, x4, #0x8
   adrp lr, X64ReturnInstr
   ldr lr, [lr, #:lo12:X64ReturnInstr]
   br x17
